# Coding standards

## One unit per file

Each file has exactly one public class, widget, or enum. Soft limit ~200 lines; split by responsibility above that. No `utils` files of top-level functions.

## Classes over functions

Behavior lives in a class. Top-level functions only for `main()` and trivial private one-liners. Widgets are `StatelessWidget` / `StatefulWidget` classes, not `Widget buildX()` helpers.

```dart
// BAD — loose helpers and a god file
Widget buildToolbar() { ... }
void clipStroke() { ... }

// GOOD
class DrawingToolbar extends StatelessWidget { ... }
class RegionEngine { bool contains(Offset point) { ... } }
```

## SOLID here

- One reason to change per class (`StrokeRenderer` renders; `RegionEngine` hit-tests).
- New tool = new class in `core/tools/` that implements `DrawingTool`. No new `switch` in the engine or ViewModel.
- Repository interfaces must be swappable (fake in tests, real in app).
- Small interfaces (`ArtworkReader`, `ArtworkWriter`), not one god type.
- Constructors receive abstractions. Only `app/app.dependencies.dart` constructs `Dio`, repos, and ViewModels.

## Naming

Files: `<name>.<role>.dart` — `drawing.screen.dart`, `drawing.viewmodel.dart`, `pencil.tool.dart`, `artwork.api.dart`.
Classes: `DrawingScreen`, `DrawingViewModel`, `PencilTool`, `ArtworkRepository` + `ArtworkRepositoryImpl`.
